    "reading": {
        "body": "New Mexico's 2026 legislative session produced 36 tracked bills, and the ones that passed are almost entirely health licensure compacts, not AI rules.\n\nThe AI bills are real but early. The Artificial Intelligence Accountability Act, the Artificial Intelligence Government Use Act, the Artificial Intelligence Transparency Act, the Chatbot Safety Act, and an AI ethics school elective were all introduced and none show a status beyond that. The same is true of the data bills that would matter most to AI systems: the Data Broker Privacy Act, the Consumer Information and Data Protection Act, and the Voice and Visual Likeness Rights Act. What passed instead were appropriations and capital outlay, plus a run of interstate medical, nursing, dental, and therapy licensure compacts. A legislature can pass a compact in weeks and still be debating what an AI model owes a user.\n\nThe enforcement pressure is arriving through courts and the attorney general rather than statutes. A jury found Facebook deceived users over data privacy, and the state is seeking up to $40 billion from Meta in that case. The attorney general and a state lawmaker have announced a push to rein in rogue AI models. That is the pattern worth noting: where the legislature has not yet acted, litigation and executive attention are setting the terms.\n\nThe one item that touches compute directly is federal, not state. Senator Heinrich's GRID Savings Act would force AI data centers to pay for grid upgrades, and the coverage frames it against a competing bill that falls short. That is the only entry in this data connecting AI to power and buildings, and it is a proposal, not a law. Nothing here shows a data center built, a megawatt contracted, or a utility agreement signed in New Mexico.\n\nWhat this data does not cover: it does not say whether any introduced bill died, whether the Meta case has been decided, or what the $40 billion figure represents beyond a sought amount. It lists titles and statuses, not text, so the strength of any AI provision cannot be judged from it.",
